On Sunday 23 November 2003 21:57 Dan Anderson wrote:

>         I'd successfully  managed to descend  6 levels when,  while my
> cat was ahead of me, I heard an explosion in the distance and couldn't
> find  my cat  after searching  the entire  level several  times.  What
> happened to my pet?

Did you have any sad feelings? If not, it probably fell through a trap
door or tripped a level teleporter. If you did, chances are that your
cat stepped on a land mine (that's what the explosion was) and was
killed.
 
>         Also, how  do I tell what level  my cat is?  

With a stethoscope (stand next to it and apply the stethoscope in its
direction) or a wand of probing (zap in its direction from not too far,
no need to be adjacent), neither of which bothers it.

> It  just seems to
> kill things and changes form a kitten to a housecat, but little else.

It will grow to a large cat yet and stop at, IIRC, level 9. Pets gain
experience with every kill.
 
>         If my  cat is  gone for good  (which I  assume it is,  since a
> homonulus (or whatever they're called)  

Homunculus, "little man", a kind of minor demon. Green thingy, you to
sleep, may give sleep resistance when eaten but its meat is poisonous.

> was kind enough to telelport /
> dig me down a  level), 

Monsters can't level teleport you, so it probably zapped a wand of
digging through the floor and you fell through the hole after it.

> if I see another cat in  the dungeon is there a
> way for me to make a pet out  of it?  

Yes. Throw some food it will eat at it. Tripe, meatballs, food rations,
pancakes, fortune cookies, cram, lembas, soldiers' rations, fresh meaty
corpses and fresh or dried lizards all work.

> Is it even worth it, or should I seriously consider doing it?

If you like having a pet, do it by all means; it doesn't take much of
your resources and it isn't hard. You can tame a dog with the same kind
of food, or a horse (pony, horse, warhorse) with fruit, veggies or
lichen corpses.

Throwing food a pet-type animal can't eat at it (tins, fruit to cats or
dogs, meat to horses) will make it peaceful and you can come back to
tame it later.

Most monsters, including  non-pet types, can also be tamed with a scroll
of taming or a charm monster spell.

